{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And He gave to Moses.</b> Its sense is: ויתן אלהים אל משה — 'and Elohim gave to Moses'; and it draws upon the Name mentioned at the end of the verse: באצבע אלהים — 'by the finger of Elohim.' For the utterance at Sinai, the making of the tablets, and the inscription — all was in the name Elohim, which is the final ה of the Name. And I have already mentioned this in the comment on וידבר." "<b>And already</b> you know that every name Elohim mentioned in the work of Creation is the תשובה (return/repentance), and what He afterward mentioned (Genesis 2:4) — בהבראם בה' בראם — that is this final ה, for the ה created with the power of תשובה. And lest you think that it is the principal and the cause beyond which there is none higher, therefore it is זעירא (small). And therefore He immediately appended: ביום עשות ה' אלהים — He appended the making to the special Name, which is the principal. And so David, peace be upon him, mentioned (Psalms 145:9): ״טוב ה' לכל ורחמיו על כל מעשיו״ — he said this concerning the special Name, and therefore spoke in the third person; and afterward he mentioned: ״יודוך ה' כל מעשיך״ — he said this concerning the attribute of judgment, and therefore spoke in the second person. And this is the meaning of ״וחסידיך יברכוכה״ — full with the ה, and appended to it: ״כבוד מלכותך״ — for the name יה is the attribute of judgment, in the manner of (Exodus 15:2): ״עזי וזמרת יה״, and as I have mentioned there." "<b>When He had finished.</b> It is written ככלתו (as a bride) — for the Torah was given to him as a gift, like a bride who is given to the groom; for were it not so, he would not have been able to learn the Torah in forty days." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> And was the entire Torah as a bride learned by Moses in forty days? But is it not written (Job 11:9): ״ארוכה מארץ מדה ורחבה מני ים״? Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, taught him the general principles. Said Resh Lakish: anyone who puts forth words of Torah and they are not pleasing to those who hear them, like this bride who is pleasing to her husband — it would have been better for him had he not said them. For at the hour the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Torah to Israel, it was beloved to Israel like this bride who is beloved to her husband. Said Rabbi Shimon: just as this bride requires twenty-four kinds of adornment, as stated (Genesis 2:22): ״ויבאה אל האדם״ — from where? ויבא״ה — to teach that the Holy One, blessed be He, adorned Eve with twenty-four kinds of adornments, and they are spoken of in Isaiah; so too a Torah scholar must be expert and adorned in twenty-four books. And likewise the Sabbath, which is called a bride — we find that Rav Ashi composed tractate Shabbat in twenty-four chapters. And the reason the Sabbath is called a bride is that the Sabbath is the end of the six days of Creation and the ultimate purpose of the making of heaven and earth; for thus the Sabbath above is the end of the אצילות. And so too the custom of this physical world is that the bride is sought last; and so Solomon, peace be upon him, commanded (Proverbs 24:27): ״ועתדה בשדה לך אחר ובנית ביתך״, and the word אחר is an imperative, like (Psalms 55): ״פלג לשונם״." "<b>Two tablets of the testimony.</b> They were a testimony to Israel that the Shekhinah dwells among them, like one who brings the king's seal in his hand. And by way of the plain sense (peshat): the reason they were not one tablet upon which the Ten Utterances could be written, but were two, is because they are called לוחות העדות, 'tablets of testimony,' and there is no testimony except by two. And furthermore: לוחות העדות — the tablets were from the earth, and the writing was from heaven, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 31:28): ״ואעידה בם את השמים ואת הארץ״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'two tablets of the testimony' — corresponding to heaven and earth; corresponding to bridegroom and bride; corresponding to two attendants; corresponding to two worlds. Rabbi Ḥanina said: it is written לחת ('tablets') — neither one greater than the other. And why of stone? Because most of the punishments in them are by stoning. Another interpretation: 'tablets of stone' — given in the merit of Jacob our father, who was called 'stone,' as it is said (Genesis 49:24): משם רועה אבן ישראל. Another interpretation: in the merit of the Temple, as it is said (Isaiah 28:16): הנני יסד בציון אבן. End of quote." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the reason the tablets were two we have already written in the verse וידבר. And the expression שני תורה (i.e., the word שני as in 'two of') means that they were equal, in the manner of שני כבשים, שני השעירים. And you must contemplate that even though they were equal in their very substance, one was greater in what was inscribed upon it — and so it should be, for so the attribute of judgment receives effluence and drawing-down from the attribute of mercy. And you will find in the first tablet the Ineffable Name in each and every utterance, but you will not find it at all in the second tablet; rather, there were inscribed in it the count of the Ineffable Name — כ״ו (26) words — and this is clear." "<b>And know</b> that the tablets were square — six handbreadths in length and six handbreadths in width. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Bava Batra, chapter HaShutafin: 'The tablets — their length was six and their width six and their thickness three.' And it has already been clarified to you that with this reckoning the tablets were sealed with the seal of the King, since upon them were the Ten Commandments — five on this tablet and five on that tablet. And understand this." "<b>And if</b> you will gain further insight into the measure of the tablets — length and width and thickness — you will find that in each and every tablet there were ק״ח (108) handbreadths, and the mnemonic sign for this is (Exodus 15:25): שם שם לו ח״ק ומשפט; and in the two tablets together רי״ו (216) handbreadths. And this corresponded to the רי״ו (216) letters of the ע״ב (72) names that are drawn from the permutation of the Ineffable Name, and the mnemonic sign is (Exodus 34:6): ויעבור HaShem על פניו — for everything was expounded to Moses at Sinai. And we have grasped from this that when the tablets were placed in the Ark, the Ineffable Name was there together with all its permuted letters." "<b>And it is known</b> that the tablets were of sapphire and were taken from the Throne of Glory. And so we find regarding the Throne of Glory: כמראה אבן ספיר דמות כסא. And the word לחת, by the Atbash cipher, is כסא. Therefore the Shekhinah dwells upon them as upon a throne. And because the tablets were taken from the Throne of Glory, the Torah is called כבוד ('glory'), as it is said (Proverbs 3:35): כבוד חכמים ינחלו. And likewise the rational soul — its root is in the Throne of Glory, and it is called כבוד, as it is said (Psalms 30:13): למען יזמרך כבוד. And the soul of a person who engages in Torah merits to return to its root, as it is said (Psalms 19:7): תורת HaShem תמימה משיבת נפש."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {3 "The verse citations for Psalms 55 appear without a verse number in the source; the reference פלג לשונם corresponds to Psalms 55:10. The method-header 'על דרך המדרש' is embedded in the lemma rather than set as a separate header.", 9 "Comment [0] opens with 'וע״ד המדרש' in the lemma rather than the standard 'על דרך המדרש'; the text appears to be a continuation from a preceding comment. Similarly comment [1] opens 'וע״ד הקבלה' rather than 'על דרך הקבלה.' Rendered the method-header in each case according to the fixed register while preserving the 'and' of continuation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:34.030899838Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}